{
 "lead": "A three-phase controlled rectifier replaces the diodes of a three-phase rectifier with thyristors and delays every gate pulse by the firing angle α. With three supplies 120° apart the output has three or six pulses per cycle instead of two, so the ripple is smaller and higher in frequency and the load is shared by all three lines. These converters, the three-pulse and six-pulse circuits, feed large DC motor drives, electrolysers, battery charging stations and the terminals of HVDC links.",
 "prereq": "You need: the three-phase diode bridge and its conduction pairs from <a href=\"#voltage-doubler-and-three-phase-diode-bridge-rectifiers\">three-phase diode bridge rectifiers</a>, and the firing angle and continuous current from <a href=\"#single-phase-half-and-fully-controlled-rectifiers\">single-phase controlled rectifiers</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Pulse number and the firing reference",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Let the phase voltages be va = Vm sin θ, vb = Vm sin(θ − 120°) and vc = Vm sin(θ − 240°), where Vm is the phase peak in volts, Vph = Vm/√2 the phase RMS and VL = √3 Vph the line RMS. The <b>pulse number</b> p is the number of output pulses per supply cycle; the lowest ripple frequency is p f. The single-phase bridge has p = 2, the three-phase half-wave circuit p = 3 and the three-phase bridge p = 6."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The natural firing point is no longer the zero crossing. A thyristor on phase a can conduct only once va is the most positive phase, which with these definitions first happens at θ = 30°, where va crosses vc. So α is measured from θ = 30° for T1: at α = 0 the converter behaves exactly like the diode rectifier. Measuring α from the zero crossing of va is the classic error."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Three-pulse (half-wave) circuit",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Three thyristors, one per phase, with their cathodes joined; the load returns to the star point of the transformer. Each thyristor conducts for 120° when the current is continuous, and is turned off when the next phase's thyristor is fired, because that phase is then more positive."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Vdc = (3√3 Vm / 2π) cos α = 1.17 Vph cos α",
     "where": "valid for continuous current. With an R load the current is continuous only up to α = 30°; above it Vdc = (3Vm/2π)[1 + cos(α + 30°)], zero at α = 150°."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The neutral carries the DC load current, so the transformer core sees a DC flux; a zig-zag secondary avoids it. The circuit is cheap but is mainly a teaching step towards the bridge."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Six-pulse fully controlled bridge",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Six thyristors: T1, T3, T5 on phases a, b, c form the upper (common-cathode) group, T4, T6, T2 the lower (common-anode) group. The numbering follows the firing order: a new thyristor is fired every 60°, in the order T1, T2, T3, T4, T5, T6, and the conducting pairs are T6T1, T1T2, T2T3, T3T4, T4T5, T5T6. Each thyristor conducts for 120°, and every pair puts one line voltage across the load, so a thyristor needs double gate pulses (or one 120° pulse) to start the bridge."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Vdc = (3√3 Vm / π) cos α = (3√2 VL / π) cos α = 1.35 VL cos α = 2.34 Vph cos α",
     "where": "valid for continuous current; Vm is the phase peak, VL the line RMS and Vph the phase RMS, in V. The 1.35 already contains √2 and √3. The output repeats every 60°, so the average is (3/π) ∫ √3 Vm sin(θ + 30°) dθ over one 60° window starting at 30° + α."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Vdc is 1.35 VL at α = 0, zero at 90° and negative beyond, which needs a DC source to keep the current going, exactly as in the single-phase bridge. With an R load the current is continuous up to α = 60°. Above that a line voltage reaches zero before the next thyristor is fired, the output rests at zero, and Vdc = (3√2 VL/π)[1 + cos(α + 60°)], falling to zero at 120°. This is <i>higher</i> than the cosine formula, which under-predicts the output whenever the current is discontinuous."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The <b>half-controlled three-phase bridge</b> uses three thyristors and three diodes: Vdc = (3√2 VL/2π)(1 + cos α), never negative, with freewheeling built in. With continuous current each line carries 120° blocks of ±Id, the same quasi-square wave as the diode bridge but shifted by α, so the input displacement factor is cos α (see <a href=\"#performance-displacement-factor-ripple-and-harmonics\">performance</a>)."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Circuit",
      "Pulses p",
      "Devices",
      "Vdc, continuous current",
      "Ripple"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "1-phase half-wave",
       "1",
       "1",
       "—",
       "f"
      ],
      [
       "1-phase bridge",
       "2",
       "4",
       "0.90 Vs cos α",
       "2f"
      ],
      [
       "3-phase half-wave",
       "3",
       "3",
       "1.17 Vph cos α",
       "3f"
      ],
      [
       "3-phase bridge",
       "6",
       "6",
       "1.35 VL cos α",
       "6f"
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A three-phase fully controlled bridge is fed from a 400 V, 50 Hz line, with continuous load current, at α = 30°. Find (a) Vdc, (b) the lowest ripple frequency and (c) Vdc of a three-pulse circuit on the same supply at the same α.",
   "steps": [
    "(a) Vd0 = 3√2 × 400/π = 1697.06/3.1416 = 540.19 V. Vdc = 540.19 × cos 30° = 540.19 × 0.8660 = 467.82 V.",
    "(b) p = 6, so the lowest ripple frequency is 6 × 50 = 300 Hz.",
    "(c) Vph = 400/√3 = 230.94 V and Vm = √2 × 230.94 = 326.60 V. Vdc = (3√3 × 326.60/2π) × 0.8660 = 270.09 × 0.8660 = 233.91 V, exactly half the bridge value."
   ],
   "answer": "(a) 467.8 V; (b) 300 Hz; (c) 233.9 V."
  },
  {
   "question": "The same bridge feeds a pure 10 Ω resistance at α = 75°. Find Vdc and Id, and compare with the continuous-current formula.",
   "steps": [
    "α = 75° is above 60°, so with R the current is discontinuous.",
    "Vdc = 540.19 × [1 + cos(75° + 60°)] = 540.19 × (1 − 0.7071) = 540.19 × 0.2929 = 158.22 V.",
    "Id = 158.22/10 = 15.82 A.",
    "The cosine formula gives 540.19 × cos 75° = 540.19 × 0.2588 = 139.81 V, 18.4 V too low: it counts negative slices that a resistive load cannot produce."
   ],
   "answer": "Vdc = 158.2 V and Id = 15.8 A; the continuous-current formula would wrongly give 139.8 V."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Measuring α in a three-phase circuit from the zero crossing of the phase voltage. It is measured from the crossing of two phase voltages, 30° later.",
  "Putting phase voltage into 1.35 VL cos α or line voltage into 1.17 Vph cos α.",
  "Saying each thyristor of the bridge conducts for 60°. Each conducts 120°; a new pair forms every 60°.",
  "Using 1.35 VL cos α for an R load above α = 60°. The output is then (3√2 VL/π)[1 + cos(α + 60°)], higher than the cosine value.",
  "Expecting a neutral connection for the six-pulse bridge. Only the three-pulse circuit needs one."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "What is the pulse number, and what is it for the single-phase bridge, the three-phase half-wave circuit and the three-phase bridge?",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "The number of output pulses per supply cycle: 2, 3 and 6."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Give the firing order and the conducting pairs of a six-pulse bridge.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "Firing order T1, T2, T3, T4, T5, T6 at 60° intervals; pairs T6T1, T1T2, T2T3, T3T4, T4T5, T5T6, each thyristor on for 120°."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why is α in a three-phase circuit measured from 30° after the zero crossing of the phase voltage?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "A thyristor can start only when its phase is the most positive (upper group) or most negative (lower group). Phase a becomes the most positive at θ = 30°, where it crosses phase c, so that is where a diode would start and where α = 0 lies."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A six-pulse bridge runs from a 415 V, 50 Hz line with continuous current at α = 45°. Find Vdc.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Vd0 = 3√2 × 415/π = 560.45 V. Vdc = 560.45 × 0.7071 = 396.3 V."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A three-pulse circuit has Vph = 230 V and continuous current at α = 60°. Find Vdc.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Vd0 = 3√3 × √2 × 230/(2π) = 268.99 V. Vdc = 268.99 × 0.5 = 134.5 V."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "A six-pulse bridge feeds a DC motor that is to be braked regeneratively. What must change, and why is the bridge able to do it?",
   "marks": 5,
   "a": "The current direction cannot change, so the motor's back EMF must reverse (reverse the field or the armature connections) and α must be set above 90° so that Vdc is negative. Power Vdc × Id is then negative: it flows from the machine through the bridge into the AC supply."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Why does the continuous-current formula under-predict Vdc for a resistive load at large α?",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "With R the current stops when the line voltage across the conducting pair reaches zero, so the output rests at zero instead of following the line voltage negative. Removing the negative slices raises the average above 1.35 VL cos α: at α = 75° on 400 V it is 158 V instead of 140 V."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. Two six-pulse bridges fed from star and delta secondaries form a twelve-pulse converter; the 5th and 7th harmonics cancel, which is why every HVDC terminal is built from twelve-pulse groups. Modern drives often replace the thyristor bridge with a PWM active rectifier. Search for 'twelve-pulse converter', 'HVDC valve group' and 'active front end' next.",
 "figure": {
